{# The HTML alternative. Its plain-text twin, `reset_password.txt.twig`, is the mandatory one — see
   the comment at the top of that file and `TwigPasswordResetMailer`.

   IT DOES NOT EXTEND `base.html.twig`, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. The layout is a *web page*: it
   emits an importmap, a favicon data-URI, a flash-message region and a hot-reload script. None of
   that means anything in an inbox, half of it is actively harmful there — an external `<script>` is
   a spam signal and will be stripped — and `app.request` does not exist when a Messenger worker
   renders this. An email is a different medium that happens to share a syntax.

   Email HTML is also a decade behind web HTML on purpose: Gmail strips `<head>`, so styling is
   inline; Outlook renders through Word's engine, so layout is tables when it is anything. This
   message is a heading, four paragraphs and a link, so it needs none of that machinery.

   No images, no tracking pixel, no web fonts, no CSS from anywhere. A message that fetches nothing
   renders identically with remote content blocked (the default in most clients) and has nothing to
   leak the recipient's reading habits with — which on a password-reset mail is not a nicety: a
   tracking pixel would tell whoever loaded it that this address is live and that its owner is
   currently trying to get back into an account. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <title>Reset your muzbar password</title>
    </head>
    <body style="font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: #222;">
        <h1 style="font-size: 20px;">Reset your muzbar password</h1>

        {# The only address rendered anywhere in this message, and it is the recipient's own. Twig
           autoescapes it, which matters more here than on a normal page: an address is user-supplied
           and this is the one piece of dynamic text in the file other than the link. #}
        <p>Somebody asked to reset the password for the muzbar account with this address:
            <strong>{{ recipientEmail }}</strong>.</p>

        <p>Open the link below to choose a new password:</p>

        {# The full URL is the visible link text, not "Reset password". Three practical reasons, and
           the first is worth more on this message than on any other: a recipient can see where the
           link goes before following it, which is precisely the habit phishing relies on people not
           having — and a password-reset mail is the single most impersonated message on the
           internet. Second, a client that strips the anchor still leaves something copy-pasteable,
           which the text part also guarantees. Third, a link whose text and href differ is itself a
           spam-filter signal. It is long and slightly ugly; that is a fair price. #}
        <p><a href="{{ resetUrl }}">{{ resetUrl }}</a></p>

        {# `lifetime` is a phrase built by the mailer from `PasswordResetRequest::LIFETIME_SECONDS`,
           never typed here, so changing the constant cannot leave this sentence lying. The timezone
           is pinned explicitly — see the identical note in the text template for why the filter's
           default would eventually make the word "UTC" untrue. #}
        <p>The link can be used once and expires in {{ lifetime }}, at
            {{ expiresAt|date('Y-m-d H:i', 'UTC') }} UTC.
            If it has already expired, ask for a new one from the sign-in page.</p>

        {# The line the whole message is built around, and the reason it can afford to be this calm.
           Someone who did not do this is worried by the time they have read the subject, and the
           honest, reassuring and *accurate* thing to tell them is that nothing has happened yet and
           nothing will unless they act. Note what is absent: no mention of the current password, no
           username, no detail about the account beyond the address above, and no credential of any
           kind. #}
        <p>If you did not request this, you can ignore this email &mdash; your password has not been
            changed.</p>

        <hr>

        <p style="color: #666; font-size: 13px;">This is an automated message from muzbar. Replies to
            it are not read.</p>
    </body>
</html>
